[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-4,xmax=4,ymin=-3,ymax=9,xtick={-4,-3,...,4},ytick={-2,0,...,8},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-3.1:3.1,samples=100] {x*x-1};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,-1) (-1,0) (1,0)};
\node[below right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:0,-1) {$(0,-1)$};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Since $k=1>0$, the parabola is concave up.
Vertex $(0,-1)$; axis of symmetry $x=0$; $y$-intercept $(0,-1)$.
$x^2-1=0$
$x^2=1$
$x=\pm1$
The $x$-intercepts are $(-1,0)$ and $(1,0)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-4,xmax=4,ymin=-6,ymax=6,xtick={-4,-3,...,4},ytick={-6,-4,...,6},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-3.1:3.1,samples=100] {-x*x+4};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,4) (-2,0) (2,0)};
\node[above right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:0,4) {$(0,4)$};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Since $k=-1<0$, the parabola is concave down.
Vertex $(0,4)$; axis of symmetry $x=0$; $y$-intercept $(0,4)$.
$-x^2+4=0$
$x^2=4$
$x=\pm2$
The $x$-intercepts are $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-3,xmax=3,ymin=0,ymax=13,xtick={-3,-2,...,3},ytick={0,3,...,12},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-2.2:2.2,samples=100] {2*x*x+3};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,3)};
\node[above right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:0,3) {$(0,3)$};
\end{axis}\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Since $k=2>0$, the parabola is concave up.
Vertex $(0,3)$; axis of symmetry $x=0$; $y$-intercept $(0,3)$.
$2x^2+3=0$
$2x^2=-3$
A square can never be negative, so there are no $x$-intercepts.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}\begin{axis}[width=6cm,height=6cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-4,xmax=4,ymin=-4,ymax=7,xtick={-4,-3,...,4},ytick={-4,-2,...,6},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
